## Understanding Expectations

### What are Expectations?

**Expectations** are rules that define what "good data" looks like. Each expectation checks one aspect of your data.

### Expectation Categories

#### 1. Column Expectations

Check individual columns for issues.

**Null Checks**:
```rust
.expect(no_nulls("email"))           // No null values allowed
.expect(nulls_below_percent("phone", 0.05))  // At most 5% nulls
```

**Uniqueness Checks**:
```rust
.expect(unique("id"))                // All values must be unique
.expect(unique_above_percent("email", 0.95))  // At least 95% unique
```

**Type Checks**:
```rust
.expect(is_type("age", DataType::UInt8))
.expect(is_numeric("salary"))
.expect(is_string("name"))
```

**Range Checks**:
```rust
.expect(in_range("age", 0..=120))    // Inclusive range
.expect(greater_than("salary", 0.0))
.expect(less_than("age", 150))
```

**Pattern Matching**:
```rust
.expect(matches_regex("email", r".+@.+\\..+"))
.expect(starts_with("phone", "+1"))
.expect(ends_with("website", ".com"))
```

**Specialized Formats**:
```rust
.expect(is_email("email"))
.expect(is_url("website"))
.expect(is_uuid("user_id"))
.expect(is_date("created_at", Some("%Y-%m-%d")))
.expect(is_ip_address("ip"))
.expect(is_phone_number("phone"))
```

**Value Sets**:
```rust
.expect(in_set("status", ["active", "inactive", "pending"]))
.expect(not_in_set("email", ["test@example.com", "admin@example.com"]))
```

**Length Checks**:
```rust
.expect(length_in_range("name", 2..=50))
.expect(min_length("name", 2))
.expect(max_length("name", 50))
```

#### 2. Row Expectations

Check the entire dataset.

```rust
use dataval::expectations::row::*;

.expect(count_equals(1000))              // Exactly 1000 rows
.expect(count_in_range(900..=1100))      // Between 900 and 1100 rows
.expect(count_at_least(100))             // At least 100 rows
.expect(count_at_most(10000))            // At most 10,000 rows
```

#### 3. Statistical Expectations

Check statistical properties.

```rust
use dataval::expectations::statistical::*;

.expect(mean_in_range("salary", 40000.0..=100000.0))
.expect(median_in_range("age", 25.0..=40.0))
.expect(stddev_in_range("score", 0.0..=20.0))
.expect(percentile_in_range("salary", 95, 80000.0..=150000.0))
```

#### 4. Anomaly Detection Expectations

Find outliers automatically.

```rust
use dataval::expectations::anomaly::*;

.expect(detect_zscore("amount", 3.0))        // Z-score > 3 is anomaly
.expect(detect_iqr("revenue", 1.5))          // IQR method
.expect(detect_modified_zscore("salary", 3.5))
```

#### 5. Schema Expectations

Validate data structure.

```rust
use dataval::expectations::schema::*;

.expect(has_column("id"))
.expect(has_columns(vec!["id", "name", "email"]))
.expect(matches_schema(expected_schema))
```

### Combining Expectations

```rust
let validator = Validator::new()
    // Column expectations
    .expect(no_nulls("id"))
    .expect(unique("id"))
    .expect(no_nulls("email"))
    .expect(is_email("email"))
    .expect(in_range("age", 0..=120))

    // Row expectations
    .expect(count_at_least(100))

    // Statistical expectations
    .expect(mean_in_range("salary", 40000.0..=100000.0))

    // Anomaly detection
    .expect(detect_zscore("salary", 3.0));
```

