## 5. Validation

### 5.1 Built-in Validators

```rust
use serialize_rs::Validate;

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize, Validate)]
struct User {
    #[validate(email)]
    email: String,

    #[validate(url)]
    website: String,

    #[validate(length(min = 3, max = 20))]
    username: String,

    #[validate(range(min = 18, max = 120))]
    age: u32,

    #[validate(pattern = r"^[a-zA-Z0-9_]+$")]
    handle: String,
}
```

Available validators:
- `email` - Email address
- `url` - URL
- `uuid` - UUID
- `ipv4` - IPv4 address
- `ipv6` - IPv6 address
- `length(min, max)` - String/collection length
- `range(min, max)` - Numeric range
- `pattern(regex)` - Regex pattern
- `non_empty` - Non-empty collection
- `unique` - Unique elements

### 5.2 Custom Validators

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize, Validate)]
struct User {
    #[validate(custom = "validate_password")]
    password: String,
}

fn validate_password(password: &str) -> Result<(), ValidateError> {
    if password.len() < 12 {
        return Err(ValidateError::field_invalid(
            "password",
            "length",
            "Password must be at least 12 characters",
        ));
    }
    if !password.chars().any(|c| c.is_ascii_uppercase()) {
        return Err(ValidateError::field_invalid(
            "password",
            "uppercase",
            "Password must contain uppercase letters",
        ));
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

### 5.3 Validation During Deserialization

```rust
// Validate during deserialization
let user: User = User::from_json_validated(json)?;

// Or validate after
let user: User = User::from_json(json)?;
user.validate()?;
```

### 5.4 Multiple Validation Rules

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize, Validate)]
struct User {
    #[validate(email)]
    #[validate(length(max = 255))]
    email: String,

    #[validate(length(min = 8))]
    #[validate(pattern = r"^[a-zA-Z0-9]+$")]
    password: String,
}
```

---

## 6. Versioning

### 6.1 Versioned Structs

```rust
use serialize_rs::Versioned;

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize, Versioned)]
#[version(2)]
struct User {
    #[since(1.0)]
    id: u32,

    #[since(1.0)]
    #[rename(from = "username")]  // Was 'username' in v1
    name: String,

    #[since(2.0)]
    #[default(None)]
    email: Option<String>,
}
```

### 6.2 Automatic Migration

```rust
// Deserialize old data into new struct
let v1_json = r#"{"id":1,"username":"alice"}"#;
let user: User = User::from_json(v1_json)?;
// Works! 'username' automatically renamed to 'name'
// email gets default value None
```

### 6.3 Checking Compatibility

```rust
let is_compat = User::is_compatible_with(1);
// Returns true if v1 data can be loaded into v2 struct
```

### 6.4 Manual Migration

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize, Versioned)]
#[version(3)]
#[migrate(from = 2, using = "migrate_v2_to_v3")]
struct UserV3 {
    id: u32,
    name: String,
    email: Option<String>,
    #[default(vec!["user".to_string()])]
    roles: Vec<String>,
}

fn migrate_v2_to_v3(v2: User) -> UserV3 {
    UserV3 {
        id: v2.id,
        name: v2.name,
        email: v2.email,
        roles: vec!["user".to_string()],
    }
}
```

---

## 7. Error Handling

### 7.1 Basic Error Handling

```rust
use serialize_rs::{SerializeError, DeserializeError};

fn serialize_user(user: &User) -> Result<String, SerializeError> {
    user.to_json()
}

fn deserialize_user(json: &str) -> Result<User, DeserializeError> {
    User::from_json(json)
}
```

### 7.2 Handling Specific Errors

```rust
match User::from_json(json) {
    Ok(user) => println!("User: {}", user.username),
    Err(DeserializeError::MissingField(field)) => {
        eprintln!("Missing field: {}", field);
    }
    Err(DeserializeError::InvalidValue { field, message }) => {
        eprintln!("Invalid value for {}: {}", field, message);
    }
    Err(e) => {
        eprintln!("Error: {}", e);
    }
}
```

### 7.3 Validation Errors

```rust
match user.validate() {
    Ok(_) => println!("User is valid"),
    Err(ValidateError::FieldInvalid { field, rule, message }) => {
        eprintln!("Field '{}' failed '{}': {}", field, rule, message);
    }
    Err(ValidateError::MultipleErrors(errors)) => {
        for error in errors {
            eprintln!("Error: {}", error);
        }
    }
    Err(e) => {
        eprintln!("Validation error: {}", e);
    }
}
```

### 7.4 Contextual Errors

```rust
use serialize_rs::SerializeError;

fn save_user(user: &User, path: &str) -> Result<(), SerializeError> {
    std::fs::write(path, user.to_json()?)?;
    Ok(())
}
```

---

## 8. Streaming

### 8.1 Streaming Serialization

Serialize large datasets without loading into memory:

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use serialize_rs::StreamSerializer;
use serialize_rs::formats::JsonFormat;

let file = File::create("users.json")?;
let mut serializer = StreamSerializer::new(file, JsonFormat::new())?;

// Stream 1 million users
for i in 0..1_000_000 {
    let user = User {
        id: i,
        username: format!("user_{}", i),
        email: format!("user{}@example.com", i),
    };
    serializer.serialize(&user)?;
}

serializer.finish()?;
```

### 8.2 Streaming Deserialization

```rust
use serialize_rs::StreamDeserializer;

let file = File::open("users.json")?;
let mut deserializer = StreamDeserializer::new(file, JsonFormat::new())?;

// Process users one at a time
while let Some(user) = deserializer.next()? {
    println!("User: {}", user.username);
}
```

### 8.3 Iterator Interface

```rust
let file = File::open("users.json")?;
let mut deserializer = StreamDeserializer::new(file, JsonFormat::new())?;

// Use as iterator
for user in deserializer.iter() {
    let user = user?;
    println!("User: {}", user.username);
}
```

---

## 9. Advanced Topics

### 9.1 Custom Serialization

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct Secret(String);

impl SerializeImpl for Secret {
    fn serialize<S: Serializer>(&self, serializer: &mut S) -> Result<(), S::Error> {
        // Redact the secret
        serializer.serialize_str("***REDACTED***")
    }
}

impl DeserializeImpl for Secret {
    fn deserialize<D: Deserializer>(deserializer: &mut D) -> Result<Self, D::Error> {
        let s = String::deserialize(deserializer)?;
        Ok(Secret(s))
    }
}
```

### 9.2 Conditional Serialization

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct Config {
    #[serialize(skip)]
    password: String,

    #[serialize(skip_if = "is_empty")]
    optional_field: Option<String>,
}

fn is_empty(value: &Option<String>) -> bool {
    value.as_ref().map_or(true, |s| s.is_empty())
}
```

### 9.3 Flattening

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct Metadata {
    created_at: String,
    updated_at: String,
}

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct User {
    id: u32,
    username: String,

    #[serialize(flatten)]
    #[deserialize(flatten)]
    metadata: Metadata,
}

// JSON: {"id":1,"username":"alice","created_at":"...","updated_at":"..."}
```

### 9.4 Default Values

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct User {
    id: u32,
    username: String,

    #[deserialize(default = "default_role")]
    role: String,
}

fn default_role() -> String {
    "user".to_string()
}

// If 'role' is missing in JSON, it defaults to "user"
```

### 9.5 Renaming Fields

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct User {
    #[serialize(rename = "userId")]
    #[deserialize(rename = "userId")]
    id: u32,

    #[serialize(rename = "userName")]
    #[deserialize(rename = "userName")]
    username: String,
}

// JSON: {"userId":1,"userName":"alice"}
```

---

## 10. Common Patterns

### 10.1 API Response Wrapper

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct ApiResponse<T> {
    success: bool,
    data: Option<T>,
    error: Option<String>,
}

impl<T> ApiResponse<T> {
    fn success(data: T) -> Self {
        Self {
            success: true,
            data: Some(data),
            error: None,
        }
    }

    fn error(message: String) -> Self {
        Self {
            success: false,
            data: None,
            error: Some(message),
        }
    }
}

// Usage
let response: ApiResponse<User> = ApiResponse::success(user);
let json = response.to_json()?;
```

### 10.2 Configuration File

```rust
use serialize_rs::formats::TomlFormat;

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize, Validate)]
struct Config {
    #[validate(url)]
    api_url: String,

    #[validate(range(min = 1, max = 65535))]
    port: u16,

    #[validate(length(min = 1))]
    database_url: String,
}

// Load config
let config: Config = Config::from_file_with_format("config.toml", &TomlFormat)?;
config.validate()?;
```

### 10.3 Logging Structured Data

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct LogEntry {
    timestamp: i64,
    level: String,
    message: String,
    user_id: Option<u32>,
}

let entry = LogEntry {
    timestamp: timestamp(),
    level: "INFO".to_string(),
    message: "User logged in".to_string(),
    user_id: Some(123),
};

// Serialize to JSON for structured logging
let json = entry.to_json()?;
println!("{}", json);
```

### 10.4 Caching

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use serialize_rs::Serialize;

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct CacheEntry<T> {
    data: T,
    timestamp: i64,
    ttl: u64,
}

struct Cache<T: Serialize> {
    entries: HashMap<String, CacheEntry<T>>,
}

impl<T: Serialize + Deserialize> Cache<T> {
    fn save(&self, path: &str) -> Result<(), SerializeError> {
        let json = self.to_json()?;
        std::fs::write(path, json)?;
        Ok(())
    }

    fn load(path: &str) -> Result<Self, DeserializeError> {
        let json = std::fs::read_to_string(path)?;
        Self::from_json(&json)
    }
}
```

---

## 11. Best Practices

### 11.1 Use Appropriate Formats

- **JSON**: Web APIs, configuration, debugging
- **MessagePack/Bincode**: Performance-critical code, storage
- **YAML/TOML**: Configuration files
- **CBOR**: Constrained environments

### 11.2 Validate Input

```rust
// Always validate untrusted input
let user: User = User::from_json_validated(untrusted_json)?;
```

### 11.3 Handle Versions

```rust
// Always version your data structures
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize, Versioned)]
#[version(1)]
struct UserData {
    // ...
}
```

### 11.4 Use Streaming for Large Data

```rust
// Don't load everything into memory
let mut serializer = StreamSerializer::new(file, format)?;
for item in large_iterator {
    serializer.serialize(&item)?;
}
```

### 11.5 Provide Good Errors

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct User {
    #[validate(email, message = "Invalid email address")]
    email: String,
}
```

### 11.6 Document Your Schemas

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
/// User account information
///
/// # Fields
/// - `id`: Unique user identifier
/// - `username`: Public username (3-20 characters)
/// - `email`: Email address (must be valid)
struct User {
    id: u32,
    username: String,
    email: String,
}
```

---

## 12. Troubleshooting

### 12.1 Common Issues

**Issue: "Field not found" error**

```rust
// Solution: Use #[deserialize(default)] or make field optional
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct User {
    #[deserialize(default)]
    role: String,  // Defaults to String::default()

    email: Option<String>,  // Can be null
}
```

**Issue: "Type mismatch" error**

```rust
// Solution: Use #[deserialize(with)] for custom conversion
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct Timestamp {
    #[deserialize(with = "deserialize_ts")]
    ts: i64,
}

fn deserialize_ts<'de, D>(deserializer: D) -> Result<i64, D::Error>
where
    D: Deserializer,
{
    let s = String::deserialize(deserializer)?;
    s.parse().map_err(D::Error::custom)
}
```

**Issue: Slow serialization**

```rust
// Solution: Use binary format or zero-copy
let fast = user.to_format(&BincodeFormat::new())?;
let zero_copy = User::from_bytes_zero_copy(&bytes)?;
```

**Issue: "Validation failed"**

```rust
// Solution: Check validation rules
#[validate(length(min = 3, max = 20))]  // Must be in range
#[validate(email)]  // Must be valid email
```

### 12.2 Debugging

```rust
// Enable debug output
println!("Schema: {:#?}", User::schema());

// Validate before serializing
user.validate()?;

// Use pretty JSON for debugging
println!("{}", user.to_json_pretty()?);
```

### 12.3 Getting Help

- Check the [API documentation](https://docs.rs/serialize-rs)
- Look at [examples](https://github.com/SuperInstance/serialize-rs/tree/main/examples)
- Ask questions on [GitHub Discussions](https://github.com/SuperInstance/serialize-rs/discussions)
- Report bugs on [GitHub Issues](https://github.com/SuperInstance/serialize-rs/issues)

---

## Next Steps

Now that you've mastered the basics, explore:

- [API Documentation](03-api.md) - Complete API reference
- [Developer Guide](05-dev-guide.md) - Advanced customization
- [Examples](examples.md) - Real-world examples
- [Integration with SuperInstance tools](07-use-cases.md)

Happy serializing! 🚀

