# test-rs: Developer Guide

## Architecture Deep Dive

### Core Components

test-rs consists of several core components:

```
test_rs/
├── core/           # Core traits and types
├── runner/         # Test discovery and execution
├── assert/         # Assertion system
├── fixtures/       # Fixture management
├── param/          # Parameterized testing
├── property/       # Property-based testing
├── mock/           # Mocking framework
├── snapshot/       # Snapshot testing
├── async/          # Async test support
└── cli/            # Command-line interface
```

### Test Lifecycle

1. **Discovery Phase**
   - Scan for `#[test]` functions
   - Parse test attributes
   - Build test metadata
   - Resolve fixture dependencies

2. **Planning Phase**
   - Filter tests based on patterns/tags
   - Build execution plan
   - Allocate resources

3. **Execution Phase**
   - Setup fixtures (in dependency order)
   - Run before hooks
   - Execute test
   - Run after hooks
   - Teardown fixtures (in reverse order)

4. **Reporting Phase**
   - Collect results
   - Generate reports
   - Exit with appropriate code

### Type System Design

test-rs leverages Rust's type system for compile-time guarantees:

```rust
// Testable trait for anything that can be run as a test
pub trait Testable {
    type Output;
    fn run(self) -> Self::Output;
}

// Implement for test functions
impl<F, R> Testable for F
where
    F: FnOnce() -> R,
    R: TestResult,
{
    type Output = R;
    fn run(self) -> Self::Output {
        self()
    }
}

// Implement for async test functions
impl<F, R> Testable for F
where
    F: FnOnce() -> R,
    R: Future + Send,
    R::Output: TestResult,
{
    type Output = R::Output;
    fn run(self) -> Self::Output {
        block_on(self())
    }
}
```

## Advanced Testing Patterns

### Test Builders

Use builder pattern for complex test setup:

```rust
struct TestUserBuilder {
    name: Option<String>,
    email: Option<String>,
    age: Option<u32>,
}

impl TestUserBuilder {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            name: None,
            email: None,
            age: None,
        }
    }

    fn with_name(mut self, name: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
        self.name = Some(name.into());
        self
    }

    fn with_email(mut self, email: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
        self.email = Some(email.into());
        self
    }

    fn with_age(mut self, age: u32) -> Self {
        self.age = Some(age);
        self
    }

    fn build(self) -> User {
        User {
            name: self.name.unwrap_or_else(|| "Test User".to_string()),
            email: self.email.unwrap_or_else(|| "test@example.com".to_string()),
            age: self.age.unwrap_or(30),
        }
    }
}

#[test]
fn test_with_builder() {
    let user = TestUserBuilder::new()
        .with_name("Alice")
        .with_age(25)
        .build();

    expect(user.name).to_equal("Alice");
    expect(user.age).to_equal(25);
}
```

### Test Factories

Create factories for generating test data:

```rust
struct UserFactory;

impl UserFactory {
    fn create() -> User {
        User {
            id: 1,
            name: "Test User".to_string(),
            email: "test@example.com".to_string(),
            age: 30,
        }
    }

    fn with_email(mut user: User, email: impl Into<String>) -> User {
        user.email = email.into();
        user
    }

    fn adult() -> User {
        User {
            age: 30,
            ..UserFactory::create()
        }
    }

    fn minor() -> User {
        User {
            age: 15,
            ..UserFactory::create()
        }
    }
}

#[test]
fn test_with_factory() {
    let user = UserFactory::adult();
    expect(user.age).to_be_at_least(18);
}
```

### Test Context Objects

Use context objects to share state across tests:

```rust
struct TestContext {
    database: Database,
    user_repo: UserRepository,
    user_service: UserService,
}

impl TestContext {
    fn new() -> Self {
        let database = Database::in_memory();
        let user_repo = UserRepository::new(database.clone());
        let user_service = UserService::new(user_repo.clone());

        Self {
            database,
            user_repo,
            user_service,
        }
    }

    fn create_test_user(&self) -> User {
        self.user_service.create("test@example.com", "password")
    }
}

#[test]
fn test_with_context() {
    let ctx = TestContext::new();

    ctx.create_test_user();
    expect(ctx.user_service.count()).to_equal(1);
}
```

### Custom Assertion Matchers

Create domain-specific matchers:

```rust
trait Matcher<T> {
    fn matches(&self, value: &T) -> bool;
    fn describe(&self) -> String;
}

struct EmailMatcher;

impl Matcher<String> for EmailMatcher {
    fn matches(&self, value: &String) -> bool {
        value.contains('@') &&
        value.contains('.') &&
        !value.starts_with('@')
    }

    fn describe(&self) -> String {
        "a valid email address".to_string()
    }
}

trait EmailExt {
    fn to_be_valid_email(&self) -> AssertionResult;
}

impl EmailExt for Expectation<String> {
    fn to_be_valid_email(&self) -> AssertionResult {
        let matcher = EmailMatcher;
        if matcher.matches(&self.value) {
            Ok(())
        } else {
            Err(AssertionError {
                message: format!("Expected {} to be {}", self.value, matcher.describe()),
                location: self.location.clone(),
            })
        }
    }
}

#[test]
fn test_custom_matcher() {
    expect("user@example.com").to_be_valid_email();
}
```

