## Handling Backpressure

### Understanding Backpressure

**Problem**: Fast producer + slow consumer = unbounded memory growth

```
Producer: 1000 msg/s
Consumer:  100 msg/s
Result:    900 msg/s accumulate in queue → OOM
```

### Strategies

#### 1. Drop Oldest

```rust
let server = WebSocketServer::new()
    .with_backpressure(BackpressureStrategy::DropOldest);
```

**Use when**: Recent messages matter more than old ones (live dashboards, stock tickers)

#### 2. Drop Newest

```rust
let server = WebSocketServer::new()
    .with_backpressure(BackpressureStrategy::DropNewest);
```

**Use when**: Old messages matter more than new ones (event logs, history)

#### 3. Buffer with Limit

```rust
let server = WebSocketServer::new()
    .with_backpressure(BackpressureStrategy::Buffer(1000));
```

**Use when**: Can afford to buffer some messages

#### 4. Return Error

```rust
let server = WebSocketServer::new()
    .with_backpressure(BackpressureStrategy::Error);

// Client must handle error
match server.broadcast(message).await {
    Ok(_) => {},
    Err(Error::BufferFull) => {
        // Handle backpressure
    },
}
```

**Use when**: Need explicit control

#### 5. Wait

```rust
let server = WebSocketServer::new()
    .with_backpressure(BackpressureStrategy::Wait);
```

**Use when**: Can't drop messages, willing to block

#### 6. Custom Strategy

```rust
let server = WebSocketServer::new()
    .with_backpressure(BackpressureStrategy::Custom(Box::new(|queue_size| {
        if queue_size > 1000 {
            // Drop if too full
            BackpressureAction::Drop
        } else if queue_size > 500 {
            // Slow down producer
            BackpressureAction::Wait
        } else {
            // Buffer normally
            BackpressureAction::Buffer
        }
    })));
```

